// The numbering, as data. WORD_NUMBERS.md is the authority and this file is the
// copy a compiler can check -- when the two disagree, that file is right and
// this one is the bug.
//
// THERE IS NO GENERATOR AND NO BUILD STEP. These are X-macro lists: a consumer
// defines SAT_NODE and SAT_ALIAS, includes this file, and undefines them again.
// The enum, the node table, the spelling interner, the invariants, the digest
// and `satl --words` therefore all come from ONE list and cannot drift apart,
// which is the whole reason the file exists. Adding a word is one line here.
//
// The first satellite's registry made that same promise with NO CONSUMER for
// three commits, and four defects accumulated behind it: two sections of its
// design assigned kind 4 to different things, an id was frozen for a selector
// no receiver implemented, and one section pinned the builtin selectors to a
// literal range one commit before the map made it wrong. PLAN M2 requires a
// consumer in the milestone that writes the registry for exactly that reason --
// words.hpp, `satl --words` and tests/words_test are it.
//
// THIS FILE IS THE ONE EXEMPTION FROM PLAN §3's 300-LINE CEILING. It is data,
// and splitting a numbering whose meaning is registration order is the one
// split that could silently change what a program means (DESIGN §4.3).

// --- how a row is read ------------------------------------------------------
//
//     SAT_NODE(parent, ident, text, kind)
//
// THE NUMBER IS NOT A COLUMN. A node's number is its POSITION among its
// parent's SAT_NUMBERED children, counting from 1, in the order the rows appear
// here -- PLAN §8's "each entry names its parent, and its position among that
// parent's children IS its number." The `// 1 5 1` ending every row is a
// comment and nothing reads it; it is there so this file can be read against
// WORD_NUMBERS §2.2 by eye. What actually checks the transcription is
// tests/words_test, which opens that section of the markdown and walks all 236
// of its paths -- because a number that only a human compares is not checked.
//
// So ORDER IS MEANING here, everywhere, and DESIGN §4.3 is the whole of the
// editing discipline: never renumber, never reuse, always append. A row
// inserted in the middle silently renumbers every sibling after it, and every
// already-written program with it.
//
// `kind` is one of two things:
//
//     SAT_NUMBERED   takes the next position under its parent: 1, 2, 3, ...
//     SAT_BARE       is that parent called with NO ARGUMENTS, and is position 0
//
// SAT_BARE IS WORD_NUMBERS §1.3's `(0)`, WHICH IS A NUMBER AND NOT A NOTATION.
// `satellite.container` `1 4 (0)` says the node is 1 4 and its zero-argument
// call shape is 1 4 0 -- exactly what `satellite.include()` `1 1 0` says
// without the parentheses. §2.2 writes 35 of them as a marker on a row and 3 as
// rows of their own; that difference is spelling, so both are one form here and
// there are 38.
//
// A BARE SHAPE TAKES NO POSITION FROM ITS SIBLINGS, which is what keeps a
// parent's numbered children dense from 1 whether it has one or not -- and
// therefore what makes PLAN M2's first property, no holes, checkable as
// written rather than as an exception list.
//
// `text` is §2.2's PATH COLUMN with the parent's path removed, character for
// character. Joining a node's texts from the root reproduces the row:
//
//     satellite . console . input(prompt, target)          1 5 4
//     satellite . include (satellite)                      1 1 1
//
// A text beginning with `(` joins with NO DOT, because it is an argument and
// not a word. `satellite.include(satellite)` is include's child 1 because
// `satellite` is word 1 and extends the path (§1.3); `include(spaceship)` is
// child 2 because a user-owned argument has no number to contribute and takes
// the next slot instead. Such a row HAS NO SPELLING OF ITS OWN and is never
// walked to by name -- it is chosen by what the call hands over.
//
// THREE SPELLINGS OF `input` UNDER ONE PARENT IS CORRECT, and is the other half
// of the same rule. `input` has no number of its own anywhere in §2.2, so its
// three call shapes are siblings of `display` at 1 5 2, 1 5 3 and 1 5 4, and
// they share a spelling -- which is precisely the many-nodes-one-string case
// DESIGN §4.4 builds the interner for. `include` DOES have a number, so its
// shapes are its children instead.
//
// That is the "call shapes sit at two different depths" WORD_NUMBERS §4 asks to
// have confirmed before this file encodes it, and the two depths are one rule:
// A WORD THAT IS REACHED BARE HOLDS ITS SHAPES; A WORD THAT IS ONLY EVER CALLED
// DOES NOT EXIST APART FROM THEM.

// --- aliases ----------------------------------------------------------------
//
//     SAT_ALIAS(ident, text)
//
// A SECOND SPELLING FOR A NODE, NOT A SECOND NODE (WORD_NUMBERS §2.3), written
// relative to that node's PARENT, and it may carry a dot:
// `satellite.random.fast.range(min, max)` is four segments spelling a node that
// is three numbers.
//
// AN ALIAS TAKES NO NUMBER, so it cannot collide with one -- and that settles
// two of PLAN M2's four properties by construction instead of by assertion:
//
//   property 2, no duplicates among non-aliases. The numbering cannot express a
//     duplicate at all, because a number IS a position. §2.2's only three
//     duplicate numbers are the three `.range` rows below, and each is one node
//     here, which is exactly why that section is 236 rows carrying 233 numbers.
//   property 4, no alias points at a number that does not exist. An alias names
//     an IDENTIFIER, so the compiler rejects one that does not.
//
// Both are stronger than the asserts PLAN M2 describes, and this is why the
// alias form had to exist before the transcription could begin: an alias that
// could not be DECLARED would arrive as a duplicate number, and a check that
// cannot tell a deliberate duplicate from a typo teaches whoever hits it to
// loosen the check.

// --- the identifiers --------------------------------------------------------
//
// The path with `satellite` dropped, uppercased, dots to underscores, and an
// argument suffix ONLY where the same word is written more than once:
// CONSOLE_DISPLAY, but CONSOLE_INPUT_0 / _PROMPT / _PROMPT_TARGET.
//
// NOT THE BARE WORD, deliberately. The first satellite records why: `and`,
// `or`, `true` and `false` are C++ keywords or alternative tokens and pasting
// them into an enum name is a trap. The quoted text is what the language calls
// the thing; the identifier only has to be unique.

// --- what is in here --------------------------------------------------------
//
// Transcribed 2026-08-28 from WORD_NUMBERS §2.2 at 222 rows / 219 numbers,
// 223 / 220 since 2026-08-31, when M8 appended `digits` at `1 6 4 15`,
// 227 / 224 since 2026-09-03, when M12 appended the variant's four at
// `1 6 14 1` through `1 6 14 4`, 229 / 226 since 2026-09-05, when M16
// appended `search(pattern)` under the map at `1 4 1 10` and under the list
// at `1 4 2 26`, 231 / 228 since 2026-09-07, when the author assigned
// `persist()` `1 22 7` and `persist(x)` `1 22 8` -- WORD_NUMBERS §2.7 -- and
// 236 / 233 since 2026-09-08, when M19 appended the failed-open contract at
// `1 6 2 8`-`1 6 2 10`, the byte-exact `write(x)` at `1 6 2 11` and
// `satellite.file.exists(path)` at `1 8 5`, and 285 / 282 since 2026-09-11,
// when M20 appended 38: the arguments object's 35 -- four under `machine`,
// then `system` `build` `interpreter` `process`
// `session` with their children, then `count` -- and the three call shapes v1
// accepts that §2.2 had no number for, `swap.used(unit)` `1 22 4 4 7`,
// `this.used(unit)` `1 22 4 5 6` and `environment(name)` `1 22 9`:
//
//     295   SAT_NUMBERED   302 rows, less 3 written as bare shapes and 4 aliases
//      47   SAT_BARE       44 `(0)` markers plus those 3 rows
//     ---
//     342   nodes, each with a PathId
//      12   SAT_ALIAS      4 `.range`, `hexadecimal`, `class`, 7 of `arguments`
//
// AND THE BLOCK ABOVE WAS WRONG AGAIN THE SAME DAY, WHICH IS THE FIFTH TIME
// AND THE ONE THE PARAGRAPH BELOW PREDICTED IN AS MANY WORDS. It read
// 279 / 46 / 325 / 11 -- M20's own numbers, and M20.md §2.1 says in its own
// text that it took `kNodeCount` to 336, so this block was eleven nodes stale
// before the milestone that wrote it had finished landing. M21 found it by
// counting rather than by reading, which is the only way it could have been
// found: `grep -c '^SAT_NODE('` over this file is the whole measurement, and
// it disagreed with the line above it by 17 once M21's six were in. The
// numbers here are now 342 / 295 / 47 / 12 and they were counted that way.
// **A count in a comment still has nothing checking it** -- that is the next
// paragraph's point and it remains true -- but tests/words_test now asserts
// all four of §2.2's, so the three counts a reader most needs do have a
// checker, and this block is derivable from it in one subtraction.
//
// THE BLOCK ABOVE READ 230 / 39 / 269 UNTIL 2026-09-11 AND HAD BEEN WRONG SINCE
// M19.5. Four milestones appended nodes without touching it -- binary and hex,
// M19.6's option token, and `spacesuit` -- so it was describing a registry
// thirteen nodes smaller than the one below it. **A count in a comment has
// nothing checking it**, which is exactly the property tests/words_test gives
// §2.2's three counts and this paragraph does not have; what makes it
// recoverable is that the numbers are derivable, and `satl --words` prints the
// node total on its last line. Recorded rather than quietly fixed, because the
// next milestone to append will be the fifth to walk past it.
//
// AND FIVE SMALL LISTS OVER THOSE NODES, each a fact about a word that one pass
// needs and no other, declared on the word rather than legislated from the pass:
//
//       1   SAT_PLACE      `input(prompt, target)`'s out parameter      M14
//      30   SAT_BUILT      the front-end words help must name           M18
//       1   SAT_TOPIC      `help(x)`'s argument, which is never run     M18
//       1   SAT_DEFER      `thread.new(f(x))`'s argument, not performed M23
//       1   SAT_OPTIONS    the words WORD_NUMBERS §1.5's fold applies to M19
//
// THE WORD "FIVE" ABOVE WAS WRONG UNTIL M23 MADE IT RIGHT, WHICH IS THE FUNNIEST
// WAY THIS FILE'S STANDING PROBLEM HAS TURNED UP YET. It said FIVE and listed
// FOUR, from M19 -- when SAT_OPTIONS arrived and the prose did not move --
// until 2026-09-12, when M23 added SAT_DEFER and the sentence became true by
// accident. **A count in a comment has nothing checking it** is this file's own
// refrain, and here it was a count that would have SILENTLY HEALED: a milestone
// adding one more list and not reading the line would have left a correct
// number nobody had ever checked. The four counts beside the names are counted
// and not typed -- `grep -c '^SAT_PLACE(' words.def` and its three siblings --
// and SAT_BUILT is 30 since M23 added `satellite.variable.capsule`.
//
// Every one of them is SELF-DEFAULTING -- a consumer that does not care never
// learns the list exists -- and each says at its own head why it is data here
// instead of a comparison somewhere else.
//
// The node total is a count nobody had written down, because §2.2's 226 does not
// include the bare shape a `(0)` marker names -- the marker rides on the row of
// the node it belongs to, so it is a number that section carries without
// counting.

#ifndef SAT_NODE
#define SAT_NODE(parent, ident, text, kind)
#endif
#ifndef SAT_ALIAS
#define SAT_ALIAS(ident, text)
#endif

// The two kinds, as values, so the numbers live here and only here -- read back
// as words::kNumbered and words::kBare. NOT #undef'd at the bottom with the
// macros above, for that reason.
#ifndef SAT_NUMBERED
#define SAT_NUMBERED 1
#endif
#ifndef SAT_BARE
#define SAT_BARE 0
#endif

// The root, and the only node with no parent. It is 1 because it is the root,
// and it is also a value -- the runtime singleton, which is why
// satellite.include(satellite) and satellite.return(satellite) both mean
// something (DESIGN §3).
SAT_NODE(NONE, SATELLITE, "satellite", SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1

// --- include ----------------------------------------------------------------
// DESIGN §3 line 1. The one word with both kinds of call shape: a bare form,
// a language-owned argument, and a user-named one.
//
SAT_NODE(SATELLITE, INCLUDE,           "include",     SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 1
SAT_NODE(INCLUDE,   INCLUDE_0,         "()",          SAT_BARE)  // 1 1 0
SAT_NODE(INCLUDE,   INCLUDE_SATELLITE, "(satellite)", SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 1 1
SAT_NODE(INCLUDE,   INCLUDE_SPACESHIP, "(spaceship)", SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 1 2

// --- capsule ----------------------------------------------------------------
// the keyword that declares one. The TYPE of a deferred call is
// satellite.variable.capsule 1 6 16, which is a different node.
//
SAT_NODE(SATELLITE, CAPSULE,   "capsule", SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 2
SAT_NODE(CAPSULE,   CAPSULE_0, "()",      SAT_BARE)  // 1 2 0

// --- main -------------------------------------------------------------------
// the capsule satl calls. Its parameter is M17's.
//
SAT_NODE(SATELLITE, MAIN,   "main", SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 3
SAT_NODE(MAIN,      MAIN_0, "()",   SAT_BARE)  // 1 3 0

// --- container --------------------------------------------------------------
// the map and the list, and their 34 methods -- the map's nine 1 4 1 1-1 4 1 9
// and the list's twenty-five 1 4 2 1-1 4 2 25. PLAN M16.
//
// 34 AND NOT 29, which is the figure PLAN M16 and SCRATCH.md/MILESTONE.md both
// carried. It came from the ledger counting the list at twenty when §2.2 gives
// it twenty-five, and PLAN inherited it into a sentence that names nine and
// twenty-five two lines above. Corrected in all three on 2026-08-28.
//
SAT_NODE(SATELLITE,      CONTAINER,                     "container",       SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER,      CONTAINER_0,                   "()",              SAT_BARE)  // 1 4 0
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER,      CONTAINER_MAP,                 "map",             SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 1
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_MAP,  CONTAINER_MAP_0,               "()",              SAT_BARE)  // 1 4 1 0
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_MAP,  CONTAINER_MAP_SET,             "set(k, v)",       SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 1 1
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_MAP,  CONTAINER_MAP_GET,             "get(k)",          SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 1 2
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_MAP,  CONTAINER_MAP_HAS,             "has(k)",          SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 1 3
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_MAP,  CONTAINER_MAP_SIZE,            "size",            SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 1 4
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_MAP,  CONTAINER_MAP_EMPTY,           "empty",           SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 1 5
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_MAP,  CONTAINER_MAP_CLEAR,           "clear",           SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 1 6
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_MAP,  CONTAINER_MAP_REMOVE,          "remove(k)",       SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 1 7
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_MAP,  CONTAINER_MAP_KEYS,            "keys",            SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 1 8
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_MAP,  CONTAINER_MAP_VALUES,          "values",          SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 1 9
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_MAP,  CONTAINER_MAP_SEARCH,          "search(pattern)", SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 1 10
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER,      CONTAINER_LIST,                "list",            SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 2
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_LIST, CONTAINER_LIST_0,              "()",              SAT_BARE)  // 1 4 2 0
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_LIST, CONTAINER_LIST_APPEND,         "append",          SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 2 1
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_LIST, CONTAINER_LIST_SIZE,           "size",            SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 2 2
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_LIST, CONTAINER_LIST_SORT_0,         "sort()",          SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 2 3
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_LIST, CONTAINER_LIST_SORT_DIRECTION, "sort(direction)", SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 2 4
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_LIST, CONTAINER_LIST_SORT_DOWN_0,    "sort_down()",     SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 2 5
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_LIST, CONTAINER_LIST_SORT_DOWN_KEY,  "sort_down(key)",  SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 2 6
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_LIST, CONTAINER_LIST_SORT_UP,        "sort_up(key)",    SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 2 7
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_LIST, CONTAINER_LIST_CONTAINS,       "contains(x)",     SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 2 8
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_LIST, CONTAINER_LIST_INDEX_OF,       "index_of(x)",     SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 2 9
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_LIST, CONTAINER_LIST_EMPTY,          "empty",           SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 2 10
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_LIST, CONTAINER_LIST_CLEAR,          "clear",           SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 2 11
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_LIST, CONTAINER_LIST_FIRST,          "first",           SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 2 12
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_LIST, CONTAINER_LIST_LAST,           "last",            SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 2 13
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_LIST, CONTAINER_LIST_TRUNCATE,       "truncate(n)",     SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 2 14
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_LIST, CONTAINER_LIST_RESERVE,        "reserve(n)",      SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 2 15
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_LIST, CONTAINER_LIST_REMOVE_FIRST_0, "remove_first()",  SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 2 16
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_LIST, CONTAINER_LIST_REMOVE_LAST_0,  "remove_last()",   SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 2 17
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_LIST, CONTAINER_LIST_REMOVE_AT,      "remove_at(n)",    SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 2 18
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_LIST, CONTAINER_LIST_REMOVE,         "remove(x)",       SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 2 19
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_LIST, CONTAINER_LIST_INSERT,         "insert(n, x)",    SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 2 20
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_LIST, CONTAINER_LIST_JOIN,           "join(separator)", SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 2 21
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_LIST, CONTAINER_LIST_REVERSE,        "reverse",         SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 2 22
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_LIST, CONTAINER_LIST_SUM,            "sum",             SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 2 23
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_LIST, CONTAINER_LIST_MAX,            "max",             SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 2 24
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_LIST, CONTAINER_LIST_MIN,            "min",             SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 2 25
// The rich spelling of the search power, appended 2026-09-05 by the author's
// decision at M16 -- v1 had `.search(p)` on both containers and the 2026-08-28
// transcription carried no row for it, so the milestone that ports the power
// is the one that minted the number. One word, two rows, because a selector's
// number is reachable only through the receiver's type (WORD_NUMBERS §1.5) --
// the same reason `size` and `empty` already appear under both.
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_LIST, CONTAINER_LIST_SEARCH,         "search(pattern)", SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 2 26
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER,      CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS,           "arguments",       SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 3
// THE TEN SELECTORS, settled by the author 2026-09-11 and built at M20. This
// node is a TYPE and not a namespace -- WORD_NUMBERS §1.5's fold is what makes
// a row here reachable, through the receiver's declared type and through
// nothing else -- so these are M16's thirty-four container methods one level
// down, over the object DESIGN §7.7 hands `satellite.main`.
//
// NO `()` ROW, AND THE ABSENCE IS THE DECISION. `list()` `1 4 2 0` and `map()`
// `1 4 1 0` are constructors; there is exactly one arguments object per run and
// the language hands it over, so a program has nothing to construct. §1.3's
// bare shape is for a word that means something with no arguments, and this one
// would mean nothing at all.
//
// `length` AND `count` ARRIVE BESIDE `size` AND NOT INSTEAD OF IT, which
// DESIGN §1's "a word means one thing everywhere" would otherwise argue
// against. The reading that makes them one thing each is that this object is
// TWO containers at once: `length()` is how long the command line is and
// `count()` is how many named facts it holds, and a single `size` could only
// ever answer one of those while looking like it answered both.
//
// `count` IS ALSO A ROW UNDER `arguments` `1 14 1 1 9` AND THE REUSE IS
// DELIBERATE. That one is how many words were on the command line; this one is
// how many entries the object holds. Both are counts of what their own receiver
// holds, which is §2.3's distinction between a word and the node it hangs under.
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS, CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_LENGTH,    "length",          SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 3 1
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS, CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_COUNT,     "count",           SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 3 2
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS, CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_KEYS,      "keys",            SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 3 3
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS, CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_TO_STRING, "to_string",       SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 3 4
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS, CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_LINES,     "lines",           SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 3 5
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS, CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_HAS,       "has(k)",          SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 3 6
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS, CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_GET,       "get(k)",          SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 3 7
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS, CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_FIRST,     "first",           SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 3 8
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS, CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_LAST,      "last",            SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 3 9
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS, CONTAINER_ARGUMENTS_CONTAINS,  "contains(x)",     SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 3 10
SAT_NODE(CONTAINER,      CONTAINER_RESULT,              "result",          SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 4 4

// --- console ----------------------------------------------------------------
// DESIGN §10.1. display is M10; input and typed() are the reader thread,
// which reaches no milestone.
//
SAT_NODE(SATELLITE, CONSOLE,                     "console",               SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 5
SAT_NODE(CONSOLE,   CONSOLE_0,                   "()",                    SAT_BARE)  // 1 5 0
SAT_NODE(CONSOLE,   CONSOLE_DISPLAY,             "display",               SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 5 1
SAT_NODE(CONSOLE,   CONSOLE_INPUT_0,             "input()",               SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 5 2
SAT_NODE(CONSOLE,   CONSOLE_INPUT_PROMPT,        "input(prompt)",         SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 5 3
SAT_NODE(CONSOLE,   CONSOLE_INPUT_PROMPT_TARGET, "input(prompt, target)", SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 5 4
SAT_NODE(CONSOLE,   CONSOLE_TYPED_0,             "typed()",               SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 5 5
SAT_NODE(CONSOLE,   CONSOLE_WIDTH,               "width",                 SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 5 6
SAT_NODE(CONSOLE,   CONSOLE_HEIGHT,              "height",                SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 5 7
SAT_NODE(CONSOLE,   CONSOLE_CLEAR_0,             "clear()",               SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 5 8
SAT_NODE(CONSOLE,   CONSOLE_HOME_0,              "home()",                SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 5 9

// --- variable ---------------------------------------------------------------
// the types. string 16 methods, number 14, file 5, thread 2.
//
SAT_NODE(SATELLITE,       VARIABLE,                    "variable",              SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 6
SAT_NODE(VARIABLE,        VARIABLE_0,                  "()",                    SAT_BARE)  // 1 6 0
SAT_NODE(VARIABLE,        VARIABLE_STRING,             "string",                SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 6 1
SAT_NODE(VARIABLE_STRING, VARIABLE_STRING_0,           "()",                    SAT_BARE)  // 1 6 1 0
SAT_NODE(VARIABLE_STRING, VARIABLE_STRING_SIZE,        "size",                  SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 6 1 1
SAT_NODE(VARIABLE_STRING, VARIABLE_STRING_EMPTY,       "empty",                 SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 6 1 2
SAT_NODE(VARIABLE_STRING, VARIABLE_STRING_FIND,        "find(x)",               SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 6 1 3
SAT_NODE(VARIABLE_STRING, VARIABLE_STRING_CONTAINS,    "contains(x)",           SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 6 1 4
SAT_NODE(VARIABLE_STRING, VARIABLE_STRING_SUBSTRING,   "substring(start, end)", SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 6 1 5
SAT_NODE(VARIABLE_STRING, VARIABLE_STRING_STARTS_WITH, "starts_with(x)",        SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 6 1 6
SAT_NODE(VARIABLE_STRING, VARIABLE_STRING_ENDS_WITH,   "ends_with(x)",          SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 6 1 7
SAT_NODE(VARIABLE_STRING, VARIABLE_STRING_LOWER,       "lower",                 SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 6 1 8
SAT_NODE(VARIABLE_STRING, VARIABLE_STRING_UPPER,       "upper",                 SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 6 1 9
SAT_NODE(VARIABLE_STRING, VARIABLE_STRING_SPLIT,       "split(separator)",      SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 6 1 10
SAT_NODE(VARIABLE_STRING, VARIABLE_STRING_TRIM,        "trim",                  SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 6 1 11
SAT_NODE(VARIABLE_STRING, VARIABLE_STRING_REPLACE,     "replace(a, b)",         SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 6 1 12
