// The 31 of DESIGN §7.7's 33 that had no number, NESTED -- the author's call
// 2026-09-09, and PLAN.md §8's M20 entry carries the argument. v1 kept them
// flat and offered a third road, no registry ids at all with the bare selector
// lowering to .get(name), on the grounds that "spending a permanent registry id
// on kernel_release would be the registry recording a fact about somebody's
// machine." M18 IS WHY THAT DOES NOT HOLD: a name with no number is one
// `satl --words` cannot print and satellite.help cannot answer for, which is a
// second-class word in a language whose tie-breaker is do everything for the
// user. The cost of this road is rows, and a number is a position among its
// parent's children, so appending renumbers nothing.
//
// THE OTHER TWO WERE ALREADY HERE: `username` 1 14 1 1 3, and v1's
// `thread_count`, which is `machine.threads` 1 14 1 1 1 3.
//
// `interpreter`'s BARE SHAPE IS THE PATH and not a sub-map, which is the one
// asymmetry in this block: `machine`, `memory`, `system`, `build`, `process`
// and `session` are groupings, and `interpreter` is a thing -- the binary that
// is running -- whose natural value is where it lives, with its version and its
// library path hanging off it as properties. §1.3's rule is what allows it: a
// trailing 0 is written where a program can write the node with nothing after
// it, and this is a node that is reached both bare and as a parent.
SAT_NODE(LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS,             LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_SYSTEM,                          "system",                SAT_NUMBERED)  // 1 14 1 1 4
SAT_NODE(LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_SYSTEM,      LIBRARY_MAIN_ARGUMENTS_SYSTEM_0,                        "()",                    SAT_BARE)  // 1 14 1 1 4 0
